**How to implement.** The relative error and the per-parameter recovery errors move into `MuellerFitting` (the S10.1 brief 'RelativeError … recoveryErrors'). This is a MOVE (.spec-md §4 'A reuse is a move or a call: the new home is created and the old body deleted in the same step'; .spec-md §7 'Harness and samples … Migration provided: a move'): after the step exactly one declaration of each moved name exists. (1) New in `MuellerFitting.fs`, after `fitAbsorbingObservations`: `type QuantityValue = | QuantityValue of double` with `member this.value`, doc one line: a recovered quantity's value in its own physical units (an index, a gyration or ε″ component, a birefringence). It exists because `RelativeError.between` took two bare `double`s beside our type (ELEV010), and steps 341 and 342 read every recovered value through it. (2) `RelativeError` (`InverseFitHarness.fs:RelativeError`) moves with `value`, and `between` becomes `static member between (actual : QuantityValue) (expected : QuantityValue) : RelativeError`, the same `|actual − expected| / |expected|` on the two values. `recoveryErrors` (`InverseFitHarness.fs:recoveryErrors`) moves after it with its signature, each axis's error being `RelativeError.between` of `QuantityValue (a.read recovered)` and `QuantityValue (a.read truth)`. Each moved declaration's doc is cut to its contract in one to three lines (AGENTS.md 'Comments: short, precise, useful'), keeping one line where it guards a hazard the code cannot show. (3) `InverseFitHarness.fs` deletes `RelativeError` and `recoveryErrors`. Until step 341 moves them, `statisticsFor`'s `bias` and `worst` call `RelativeError.between` over `QuantityValue` of the same doubles, and `describeErrors` reads the product type. `MuellerInverseTests.relativeError` becomes `RelativeError.between (QuantityValue actual) (QuantityValue expected)`. Every consumer opens the product module where the compiler asks and changes nothing else unless named here. BerremanTests' numbers are bit-identical (.spec-md §4 'BerremanTests' numbers stay unchanged'): the division runs on the same doubles. No test is added or removed. `FeasibleBiaxialInverseTests` asserts on `recoveryErrors`, the cheapest class that does.

**Acceptance.** After the move, `MuellerFitting.fs` MUST declare `QuantityValue`, `RelativeError` (whose `between` takes two `QuantityValue`s) and `recoveryErrors`, and `InverseFitHarness.fs` MUST declare neither `RelativeError` nor `recoveryErrors`; unit-tests MUST pass over the selected class, `BerremanTests.FeasibleBiaxialInverseTests`, its numbers and count unchanged; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, its count unchanged; and build, elevation and the nineteen s33 invariants MUST pass.
